            title: 'China and EU Reach Interim Deal on Electric Vehicle Tariffs',
            desc: 'After more than a year of tense negotiations, China and the EU agreed on a temporary minimum price mechanism to end the EV tariff dispute that has strained trade relations.',
            content: '<p>The European Commission and China\'s Ministry of Commerce announced in early July 2026 that both sides had reached an interim agreement on a minimum import price mechanism for Chinese-made electric vehicles exported to the EU market — ending a trade dispute that began when the EU imposed additional tariffs of up to 45.3% in October 2024.</p><p>Under the new agreement, Chinese EV manufacturers including BYD, SAIC (MG), and Geely agreed to set minimum selling prices in the European market in exchange for avoiding the previously imposed additional tariffs. The scheme resembles a similar arrangement previously applied to the China-EU solar panel dispute years earlier.</p><p>China\'s Commerce Minister Wang Wentao called the agreement a "constructive step forward" avoiding further trade war escalation, while EU Trade Commissioner Maros Sefcovic stated the deal "protects Europe\'s automotive industry while maintaining healthy trade relations with China."</p><p>However, the agreement drew criticism from several European automakers. The European Automobile Manufacturers Association (ACEA) expressed concern that the minimum price mechanism was insufficient to protect local manufacturers from price competition still favoring Chinese EVs due to production subsidies and large economies of scale. Volkswagen and Stellantis, Europe\'s two largest automakers, have reported significant EV market share losses due to Chinese competition.</p><p>The agreement will remain in effect during an 18-month evaluation period, with both sides agreeing to conduct a comprehensive review in early 2028 to determine whether the mechanism should be made permanent, revised, or fully rescinded.</p>',
